Your [[@lungs-breathe|lungs]] fill with air through your mouth and nose. Many sea cucumbers do something rather different. They pump seawater in through the anus into branching tubes called respiratory trees, take out the oxygen, then squirt the water back out.

That opening makes a handy front door for lodgers. Small, eel-like pearlfish live inside some sea cucumbers, sliding in tail first and coming out at night to feed. Tiny crabs and shrimps sometimes move in too.

{{story:earthworms}}

In the 1980s, Indian cities and villages had tens of millions of vultures. They cleaned up dead cattle within hours. Then, through the 1990s, they began dropping dead. Within about a decade, more than 95% of some species were gone.

Scientists finally traced the cause to diclofenac, a painkiller given to sick cows. When vultures ate a treated cow that had died, the medicine destroyed their kidneys. With the vultures gone, [[carcass|carcasses]] lay rotting for days. Stray dogs and rats multiplied to feed on them, which brought new health worries for the people living nearby. India banned diclofenac for animals in 2006. Today [[@vultures-india|vultures]] are being bred in centres in Haryana, Assam and elsewhere, and released slowly back into the wild.

{{story:dodo}}

ध्वनि बस हवा का तरंगों में धकेला जाना है। गहरे सुर धीरे धकेलते हैं, गहरे ढोल के लिए शायद सेकंड में 50 बार। ऊँचे सुर सेकंड में हज़ारों बार धकेलते हैं। इंसान लगभग 20 से 20,000 कंपन प्रति सेकंड तक सुन सकते हैं, हालाँकि बूढ़े लोगों की सबसे ऊँची आवाज़ें सुनने की क्षमता घट जाती है।

इयरबड के डायफ़्राम को उन सारे कंपनों से एक साथ मेल बिठाकर थिरकना पड़ता है। धीमे संगीत के लिए वह बाल की चौड़ाई से भी कम हिलता है। बड़े कान ढकने वाले हेडफ़ोनों में बड़े ड्राइवर होते हैं, जिससे गहरे बास सुरों में मदद मिलती है, कुछ-कुछ छोटे ढोल के मुक़ाबले बड़े ढोल की तरह।

{{story:ears-hear}}

In 1714, the German-Dutch instrument maker Daniel Gabriel Fahrenheit built a reliable mercury thermometer. His scale put water's freezing point at 32 degrees and boiling at 212. In 1742, the Swedish [[astronomer|astronomer]] Anders Celsius proposed a scale with 100 steps between freezing and boiling, but he put 0 at boiling. Others soon flipped it.

Today almost every country uses Celsius. The USA mainly uses Fahrenheit for weather and cooking, so a hot summer day there might be reported as 95 degrees. That is about 35°C, a very ordinary May afternoon in Delhi. The story of how the scales split is in [[@temperature-scales|temperature scales]].

